/* Mind, Reason & Knowledge (14 entries) */
DEF_ADD([
{
  id: "consciousness",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "The Argument from Consciousness",
  who: "J. P. Moreland, Consciousness and the Existence of God (2008); Richard Swinburne, The Evolution of the Soul (1986); Robert Adams",
  claim: `Conscious experience, the felt quality of seeing red or feeling pain, cannot be explained by matter in motion. Physical science describes structure and dynamics, but consciousness is something over and above these. The emergence of consciousness from unconscious matter is inexplicable on naturalism and expected on theism, since the ultimate reality is itself a conscious mind. So consciousness is evidence for God.`,
  short: `The hard problem of consciousness is real, but an unsolved problem is not evidence for a particular supernatural answer. Theism does not explain consciousness either: "a mind created minds" restates the mystery. Meanwhile, the overwhelming evidence that consciousness depends on brains (lesions, anesthesia, neurochemistry, development, decline) counts *against* minds that exist without brains. That is exactly the kind of mind God is supposed to be.`,
  points: [
    { h: "A gap is not a God", t: `Neither the theist nor the naturalist has a *mechanistic* account of why some physical processes are experienced. Every historical "only God can explain X" (lightning, disease, the variety of life, the motions of the planets) has been replaced by natural explanations. Theism does not offer a mechanism either. "God made it so" is not an explanation of *how* experience arises from anything, physical or otherwise.` },
    { h: "Mind depends on brain", t: `Every change in consciousness we can measure is correlated with a change in the brain: anesthetics switch it off, strokes remove specific capacities (language, face recognition, moral judgment), psychedelics alter it, dementia dismantles personality, and development builds it up gradually. The most economical inference is that consciousness is something brains do. That makes a disembodied, omniscient, eternal consciousness, God, the most unlike anything in our evidence (see [[disembodied-mind]] and Augustine & Martin, *The Myth of an Afterlife*, 2015).` },
    { h: "Naturalism has real options", t: `Philosophy of mind offers physicalist identity theories, functionalism, illusionism (Frankish, Dennett), and Russellian monism or panpsychism (Strawson, Goff). The last two treat experience as a fundamental feature of matter, which removes the "emergence from nothing" problem without any God. The hard problem is a research problem, not a dead end.` },
    { h: "Even dualism wouldn't get you to God", t: `If consciousness were a non-physical property, that would show that reality contains more than physics describes. It would not show that there is a personal Creator. Dualism is compatible with atheism (many Buddhists and Jains are dualist atheists), and property dualism is compatible with naturalism (Chalmers' "naturalistic dualism").` },
    { h: "Theism makes the wrong predictions", t: `If consciousness came from a divine mind, why does it appear only late, only in complex nervous systems, only after long evolutionary development, and only in a gradient (from nematodes to mammals) that tracks neural complexity? This is what we'd expect if consciousness is a biological phenomenon. It is surprising if it is a gift from a pre-existing mind who could have given it to anything.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Science will never explain qualia. It's a category error.", a: `Perhaps, though "never" has a poor track record. But even a permanent explanatory gap only shows the limits of our understanding. It doesn't give us positive evidence that a personal God exists. Panpsychism and Russellian monism, for instance, take qualia as fundamental without any God at all.`, k: "qualia never explain category error what it is like hard problem chalmers" },
    { q: "Near-death experiences prove consciousness survives without the brain.", a: `The best prospective tests of that claim, the AWARE studies, found no verified case of "seeing" hidden targets during cardiac arrest. See [[nde]].`, k: "near death experience out of body consciousness survives brain death" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Moreland, J. P. (2008). Consciousness and the Existence of God. Routledge.",
    "Chalmers, D. (1996). The Conscious Mind. Oxford University Press.",
    "Martin, M. & Augustine, K. (eds.) (2015). The Myth of an Afterlife: The Case against Life After Death. Rowman & Littlefield.",
    "Frankish, K. (2016). 'Illusionism as a Theory of Consciousness.' Journal of Consciousness Studies 23(11–12).",
    "Goff, P. (2017). Consciousness and Fundamental Reality. Oxford University Press.",
    "Koch, C. (2019). The Feeling of Life Itself. MIT Press."
  ],
  keys: "consciousness argument from mind qualia hard problem subjective experience matter can't think soul awareness materialism can't explain",
  rel: ["disembodied-mind", "soul", "nde", "argument-from-reason"]
},
{
  id: "argument-from-reason",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "The Argument from Reason and Plantinga's EAAN",
  who: "C. S. Lewis, Miracles (1947, rev. 1960); Victor Reppert, C. S. Lewis's Dangerous Idea (2003); Alvin Plantinga, Warrant and Proper Function (1993), Where the Conflict Really Lies (2011)",
  claim: `If naturalism and evolution are both true, our cognitive faculties were shaped for survival, not truth. Evolution cares about adaptive behavior, not true belief. So the probability that our faculties are reliable, given naturalism and evolution, is low or inscrutable. Anyone who accepts naturalism and evolution therefore has a defeater for all their beliefs, including naturalism itself. Naturalism is self-defeating. Lewis's version: if thoughts are just the product of non-rational causes, we have no reason to trust them.`,
  form: ["P(our faculties are reliable | naturalism & evolution) is low or inscrutable.", "Anyone who accepts N&E and sees this has a defeater for the belief that their faculties are reliable.", "That defeater spreads to every belief, including N&E itself.", "So N&E is self-defeating and cannot rationally be accepted."],
  short: `True beliefs are, on the whole, more useful than false ones for an organism that has to find food, avoid predators, and navigate a social world. So selection favors reliable belief-forming faculties within the domains that matter for survival. Plantinga's argument works only if belief *content* has no effect on behavior, which is semantic epiphenomenalism, and most naturalists reject it. Fitelson and Sober (1998) found technical problems in the probability reasoning. And we actually do find our faculties unreliable exactly where evolution predicts they would be: in abstract, statistical, and long-range reasoning.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Truth tracks fitness", t: `An animal that believes "there is a lion" when there is a lion, and acts accordingly, does better than one whose beliefs are disconnected from its surroundings. Plantinga's examples, such as a hominid who wants to be eaten but believes running away is the way to get eaten, need fantastically gerrymandered combinations of beliefs and desires. Simple, general, reliable belief formation is cheaper for an organism than vast compensating systems of error (Ramsey, 2002; Draper, 2002; Law, 2012).` },
    { h: "The argument assumes content doesn't cause behavior", t: `If the *content* of a belief, what it is about, is causally relevant to behavior, as teleosemantic theories of content hold (Millikan, Papineau), then selection acts on content and true content is favored. Plantinga's argument needs semantic epiphenomenalism, or a reductive materialism in which content "floats free" of the physical properties that cause behavior. Naturalists can reject both.` },
    { h: "Probability problems", t: `Fitelson and Sober (1998) showed that Plantinga's formal reasoning mixes up the probability of reliability given N&E in general with the probability given our total evidence. They also showed that "inscrutable" probabilities do not generate defeaters the way he claims. Once we condition on what we know about our own success (predicting eclipses, building aircraft), the reliability of our faculties is very well confirmed.` },
    { h: "We have exactly the reliability evolution predicts", t: `Our perception and everyday reasoning are good. Our intuitive statistics (base-rate neglect), probability (gambler's fallacy), physics (impetus intuitions), and agency detection (seeing faces in clouds, gods in storms) are systematically bad, as Kahneman and Tversky's research program documents. That pattern fits evolution, which built faculties for medium-sized survival problems. It fits poorly with faculties designed by an omniscient God for truth.` },
    { h: "It defeats theism too", t: `If we cannot trust our faculties without first knowing they were designed, the theist has to rely on those same faculties to arrive at "God designed my faculties." That is circular. Any skeptical argument strong enough to defeat naturalism also defeats the theist's reasoning toward God. Descartes faced this problem in the "Cartesian circle."` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "If your brain is just chemicals, why trust it to reason correctly?", a: `Because the chemicals are organized into a system that was shaped by billions of selection events in which tracking reality mattered, and because we can *check* it: our predictions about eclipses, bridges, and vaccines succeed. Being made of chemicals is not a strike against a calculator either. What matters is how a system is organized, not what it is made of.`, k: "brain just chemicals neurons trust reason atoms fizzing molecules" },
    { q: "Lewis showed that if thought is caused by non-rational processes, it can't be rational.", a: `That confuses causes with reasons. A computer's outputs are physically caused, yet it computes valid proofs, because its physical states are organized to mirror logical relations. Rational inference can be implemented in a physical mechanism; that is what computation is. Anscombe pressed Lewis on exactly this in their 1948 Socratic Club debate, and Lewis substantially revised the chapter in the second edition of *Miracles*.`, k: "lewis irrational causes non-rational process reasons causes anscombe" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Plantinga, A. (1993). Warrant and Proper Function. Oxford University Press, ch. 12.",
    "Plantinga, A. (2011). Where the Conflict Really Lies. Oxford University Press.",
    "Beilby, J. (ed.) (2002). Naturalism Defeated? Essays on Plantinga's Evolutionary Argument Against Naturalism. Cornell University Press.",
    "Fitelson, B. & Sober, E. (1998). 'Plantinga's Probability Arguments Against Evolutionary Naturalism.' Pacific Philosophical Quarterly 79: 115–129.",
    "Ramsey, W. (2002). 'Naturalism Defended.' In Beilby (ed.), Naturalism Defeated?",
    "Law, S. (2012). 'Naturalism, Evolution and True Belief.' Analysis 72(1): 41–48.",
    "Lewis, C. S. (1960). Miracles, 2nd ed., ch. 3; on the Anscombe exchange see Reppert (2003).",
    "Kahneman, D. (2011). Thinking, Fast and Slow. Farrar, Straus & Giroux."
  ],
  keys: "argument from reason plantinga eaan evolutionary argument against naturalism self-defeating can't trust brain reason lewis naturalism undermines itself reliable faculties",
  rel: ["consciousness", "math-and-logic", "transcendental"]
},
{
  id: "proper-function",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Proper Function Requires a Designer",
  who: "Alvin Plantinga, Warrant and Proper Function (1993); 'Two Dozen (or so)' arguments J and K",
  claim: `A belief counts as knowledge only if it's produced by cognitive faculties functioning properly, according to a design plan aimed at truth. "Proper function" and "design plan" are normative notions: they say how a thing is supposed to work. Naturalism can't account for how anything is supposed to work, since unguided processes just produce what they produce. So the concept of knowledge itself presupposes that we were designed, which points to a designer.`,
  short: `Philosophers of biology have a well-developed naturalistic account of proper function: a trait's function is the effect for which it was selected (the "etiological" theory of Wright, Millikan, and Neander). The heart's function is to pump blood because hearts that pumped blood were selected for doing so. Plantinga has objections to this account, but they're contested. And many epistemologists don't define knowledge in terms of proper function at all: reliabilism, virtue epistemology, and evidentialism need no design plan.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Selected-effect function", t: `Larry Wright (1973), Ruth Millikan (1984), and Karen Neander (1991) argue that a trait's proper function is what it was selected for. A malfunction is a failure to do what ancestors' traits did that caused them to be selected. This gives a natural basis for normative "supposed to" talk in biology. It's the standard account in philosophy of biology.` },
    { h: "Knowledge without design plans", t: `Plantinga's theory of warrant is one theory among many. Process reliabilism (Goldman, 1979) says knowledge is true belief produced by a reliable process; virtue epistemology (Sosa; Greco) appeals to cognitive abilities; evidentialism appeals to evidence. None needs a designer. An argument for God that depends on a contested definition of knowledge inherits its contestation.` },
    { h: "Swampman cases cut both ways", t: `Plantinga argues that a being created by chance with accurate beliefs (like Davidson's "Swampman") would lack knowledge on the etiological view, which seems wrong. But on Plantinga's own view, such a being would lack knowledge too, unless designed. Intuitions about these cases are divided and don't settle the matter.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Plantinga, A. (1993). Warrant and Proper Function. Oxford University Press, ch. 11.",
    "Millikan, R. (1984). Language, Thought, and Other Biological Categories. MIT Press.",
    "Neander, K. (1991). 'Functions as Selected Effects: The Conceptual Analyst's Defense.' Philosophy of Science 58(2): 168–184.",
    "Goldman, A. (1979). 'What Is Justified Belief?' In Pappas (ed.), Justification and Knowledge. Reidel.",
    "Walls, J. & Dougherty, T. (eds.) (2018). Two Dozen (or so) Arguments for God. Oxford University Press."
  ],
  keys: "proper function design plan warrant knowledge plantinga normative function supposed to work designer etiological function",
  rel: ["argument-from-reason", "fifth-way", "properly-basic"]
},
{
  id: "intentionality",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "The Argument from Intentionality (Aboutness)",
  who: "Alvin Plantinga, 'Two Dozen (or so) Theistic Arguments' (argument A); Franz Brentano; Victor Reppert; Greg Welty",
  claim: `Thoughts and propositions are about things: my belief that Paris is in France is about Paris. Physical objects aren't about anything; a pattern of neurons, considered as matter, is no more about Paris than a rock is. Propositions, the contents of thoughts, exist eternally and necessarily, and the only things that are essentially about something are thoughts. So propositions are best understood as the thoughts of an eternal, necessary mind: God.`,
  short: `Naturalistic theories of intentionality, such as teleosemantics (Millikan, Papineau), information-based theories (Dretske), and asymmetric dependence (Fodor), explain aboutness in terms of causal and evolutionary relations between brain states and the world. They face open problems but are active research programs. Theism doesn't explain aboutness either: it takes God's thoughts as intrinsically about things, which is the same "brute" intentionality the theist says is impossible for brains. Many philosophers who believe in propositions think they're abstract objects that don't need any mind.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Naturalized intentionality", t: `Ruth Millikan (*Language, Thought, and Other Biological Categories*, 1984) and David Papineau argue that a mental state is about what it has the biological function of tracking. Fred Dretske (*Knowledge and the Flow of Information*, 1981) grounds content in information carried by reliable causal connections. Jerry Fodor proposed asymmetric dependence. None is complete, but each offers a mechanism. "We don't yet have a complete theory" is an open problem, not evidence for God.` },
    { h: "God's thoughts are equally unexplained", t: `On the theistic view, God's thoughts are about things because they just are. That's a primitive, unexplained intentionality. If primitive intentionality is acceptable for a divine mind, the naturalist can posit primitive intentional or proto-intentional properties in nature, as some powers theorists do (Molnar's "physical intentionality"). The argument rules out brute aboutness for brains but grants it to God.` },
    { h: "Propositions don't obviously need a mind", t: `The argument also assumes that propositions must be thoughts. Frege held that they're abstract, mind-independent objects. Others reject propositions as objects altogether. William Lane Craig himself rejects the view that abstract objects are divine thoughts in favor of anti-realism (*God Over All*, 2016). See [[abstract-objects]].` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Matter can't be 'about' anything. A neuron is just a chemical process.", a: `A thermometer's reading is "about" temperature because of a reliable causal connection; a map is about a territory because of a structural correspondence. These are physical relations. Brains are vastly more complex, and representing the world is what they evolved to do. The claim that no physical system can represent anything is the conclusion of the argument, so it can't also be a premise.`, k: "matter can't be about anything neurons chemicals meaning representation" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Plantinga, A. (2007). 'Two Dozen (or so) Theistic Arguments.' In Baker (ed.), Alvin Plantinga. Cambridge University Press.",
    "Walls, J. & Dougherty, T. (eds.) (2018). Two Dozen (or so) Arguments for God: The Plantinga Project. Oxford University Press.",
    "Millikan, R. (1984). Language, Thought, and Other Biological Categories. MIT Press.",
    "Dretske, F. (1981). Knowledge and the Flow of Information. MIT Press.",
    "Fodor, J. (1990). A Theory of Content and Other Essays. MIT Press.",
    "Neander, K. (2017). A Mark of the Mental: In Defense of Informational Teleosemantics. MIT Press."
  ],
  keys: "intentionality aboutness thoughts about things propositions meaning representation brentano mind matter can't be about divine thoughts",
  rel: ["consciousness", "abstract-objects", "argument-from-reason", "meaning-reference"]
},
{
  id: "math-and-logic",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "The Laws of Logic, Mathematics, and the Order of Nature",
  who: "Presuppositional and classical apologists; Eugene Wigner's 'unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics' (1960) used as evidence by apologists; Alister McGrath; John Lennox",
  claim: `The universe is orderly, governed by precise mathematical laws, and intelligible to human minds. The laws of logic are universal, immaterial, and unchanging, so they must exist in a universal, immaterial, unchanging mind. Mathematics, a free creation of human minds, describes physical reality with uncanny precision. This "unreasonable effectiveness" is just what we'd expect if the universe and our minds came from the same rational Creator.`,
  short: `Laws of logic are not things that need a location. They are the most general constraints on any coherent description, and they would hold in any world. Making them depend on God's mind creates a new Euthyphro: could God have made contradictions true? Mathematics fits physics partly because we select and build the mathematics that fits, and partly because physics, in the relevant regimes, really is simple and regular. That calls for explanation, but a mind is not a better explanation of regularity than regularity itself.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Logic doesn't need a lawgiver", t: `"Laws" of logic are not commands that need someone to issue them. They describe what is coherent. The law of non-contradiction says no proposition is both true and false. A world in which it "failed" isn't a world we can even describe. If logic depended on God's mind, then God could presumably have thought otherwise, and contradictions could have been true. If God *couldn't* have, logic constrains God, and logic is independent of him. This is the Euthyphro dilemma again.` },
    { h: "Immaterial is not the same as mental", t: `The argument assumes that whatever is not physical must be a thought in some mind. But many philosophers who are realists about abstract objects (Frege, Gödel, contemporary platonists) hold that numbers and propositions exist independently of *any* mind. Others (nominalists, structuralists, fictionalists) hold they exist in no strong sense. None of these positions requires God.` },
    { h: "Wigner's puzzle has natural answers", t: `Mathematics is not arbitrary invention. Much of it was developed by abstracting from physical experience (counting, geometry, motion), so it fits the world because it came from the world. Mathematicians also generate vast amounts of structure, and physicists use the small part of it that fits. That is a selection effect. Hamming (1980) noted that much of physics is *not* mathematically tidy: turbulence, biology, and economics remain stubborn.` },
    { h: "Order is the default, not a miracle", t: `A universe where things behave consistently is, arguably, the minimal possibility. Disorder would also need description. On Humean views, "laws" are simply the most efficient summaries of regularities (Lewis's best-system account), so asking who enforces them is like asking who enforces the average height of Norwegians. On anti-Humean views, laws flow from the natures of physical things (Bird, Ellis). Neither requires an enforcer.` },
    { h: "Theism doesn't explain order better", t: `A God who acts in history, answers prayer, and performs miracles is a God whose world is *not* fully lawful. If the universe were less orderly, apologists could equally say this shows God's active hand. A hypothesis compatible with both order and disorder predicts neither.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "How do you account for the laws of logic in your worldview?", a: `The same way the theist does, at bottom: they are necessary, and necessary truths need no further cause. The theist says "they're grounded in God's nature," but that is just positing a necessary being whose nature happens to include logic. Saying "logic is necessary" is simpler and avoids the question of whether God could have made contradictions true. See [[transcendental]].`, k: "account for laws of logic worldview ground logic immaterial universal invariant" },
    { q: "If the universe weren't created by a rational mind, why would it be intelligible?", a: `Intelligibility is partly a fact about us. Brains evolved to model regularities, because regularities could be exploited to survive. We find the universe intelligible at our scale and remarkably *un*intuitive elsewhere (quantum mechanics, curved spacetime). The universe isn't designed to be understood. We are adapted to understand parts of it.`, k: "intelligible comprehensible rational universe understand why order science possible" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Wigner, E. (1960). 'The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Mathematics in the Natural Sciences.' Communications on Pure and Applied Mathematics 13: 1–14.",
    "Hamming, R. W. (1980). 'The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Mathematics.' American Mathematical Monthly 87(2): 81–90.",
    "Lewis, D. (1994). 'Humean Supervenience Debugged.' Mind 103: 473–490.",
    "Balaguer, M. (1998). Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics. Oxford University Press.",
    "Martin, M. (1996). 'The Transcendental Argument for the Nonexistence of God.' The Secular Web."
  ],
  keys: "laws of logic mathematics order of nature unreasonable effectiveness wigner intelligible lawgiver laws of physics who made the laws immaterial abstract",
  rel: ["transcendental", "argument-from-reason", "fine-tuning"]
},
{
  id: "abstract-objects",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Numbers, Sets, and Eternal Truths Require a Divine Mind",
  who: "Augustine, On Free Choice of the Will II; Edward Feser's 'Augustinian proof' (Five Proofs, 2017); Plantinga's arguments from collections and from numbers (B, C); Greg Welty's theistic conceptual realism; Kreeft & Tacelli's argument from truth",
  claim: `Mathematical and logical truths are eternal, necessary, and unchanging: 7 + 5 = 12 was true before any human existed and would be true if no humans had existed. Such truths are like ideas or concepts, and ideas exist in minds. A set is a collection, and collecting is something minds do. Since there are more numbers and sets than any human mind could think, they must exist in an infinite, eternal mind. The existence of necessary truths shows that God exists.`,
  short: `Philosophers of mathematics have several well-developed alternatives: platonism (abstract objects exist independently of any mind), nominalism and fictionalism (they don't exist as objects at all), and structuralism. Christian philosophers themselves disagree: William Lane Craig rejects divine conceptualism and defends anti-realism. Making numbers God's thoughts also creates a "bootstrapping" problem: if God's ideas ground all properties, then God's own properties, such as being omniscient, must exist prior to his having them.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Many naturalist options", t: `Platonists like Frege and Gödel held that mathematical objects exist necessarily and mind-independently; there's no need for them to be anyone's thoughts. Hartry Field (*Science without Numbers*, 1980) and Mark Balaguer defend nominalism and fictionalism. Structuralists treat mathematics as the study of possible structures. The argument assumes that if numbers exist, they must be mental, which only one of these views holds.` },
    { h: "Christian philosophers disagree", t: `William Lane Craig argues in *God Over All* (OUP, 2016) that the best Christian response to abstract objects is anti-realism: there are no such objects. The volume *Beyond the Control of God? Six Views on the Problem of God and Abstract Objects* (Gould, ed., 2014) has Christian philosophers defending incompatible views. If theists can't agree that abstract objects need God, the existence of mathematics isn't evidence for him.` },
    { h: "The bootstrapping problem", t: `If properties are God's concepts, then God must think the concept "omniscient" before he can be omniscient, which is circular. This "bootstrapping objection" (Bergmann & Brower, 2006; Plantinga's discussion in *Does God Have a Nature?*, 1980) is a known difficulty for divine conceptualism.` },
    { h: "Sets aren't literally collected", t: `The idea that sets need a "collector" treats a metaphor as literal. In standard set theory, the existence of sets follows from axioms, and the "collecting" intuition is a heuristic for the iterative conception of sets. Many sets, such as the uncountable set of real numbers, can't be "collected" by any finite procedure, which is a statement about their size, not about a mind.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Truth is a property of propositions, and propositions are thoughts. Eternal truth needs an eternal thinker.", a: `That's the question at issue. Frege's view that propositions are abstract objects independent of any thinker is mainstream, as is the view that "truth" is a property of sentences or beliefs that exist when there are thinkers. Neither requires an eternal mind. And if an eternal mind is needed to make 2 + 2 = 4 true, then God's thinking it must itself be true necessarily, which leaves necessary truths prior to God's thinking.`, k: "truth propositions thoughts eternal truth eternal thinker argument from truth augustine" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Augustine. On Free Choice of the Will, Book II.",
    "Feser, E. (2017). Five Proofs of the Existence of God, ch. 3.",
    "Craig, W. L. (2016). God Over All: Divine Aseity and the Challenge of Platonism. Oxford University Press.",
    "Gould, P. (ed.) (2014). Beyond the Control of God? Six Views on the Problem of God and Abstract Objects. Bloomsbury.",
    "Field, H. (1980). Science without Numbers. Princeton University Press.",
    "Balaguer, M. (1998). Platonism and Anti-Platonism in Mathematics. Oxford University Press.",
    "Bergmann, M. & Brower, J. (2006). 'A Theistic Argument against Platonism (and in Support of Truthmakers and Divine Simplicity).' Oxford Studies in Metaphysics 2: 357–386."
  ],
  keys: "abstract objects numbers sets eternal truths necessary truths mathematics needs mind augustinian proof argument from truth collections platonism divine conceptualism",
  rel: ["math-and-logic", "intentionality", "modality-grounding", "divine-simplicity"]
},
{
  id: "modality-grounding",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Possibility and Necessity Are Grounded in God",
  who: "Brian Leftow, God and Necessity (2012); Alexander Pruss, Actuality, Possibility, and Worlds (2011); Plantinga's 'argument from counterfactuals' (argument D)",
  claim: `There are facts about what's possible and necessary: it's possible that there are purple cows, necessary that 2 + 2 = 4. These modal facts need a ground. They can't be grounded in actual physical things, which are contingent and limited. They're best grounded in God: something is possible because God has the power to bring it about, and necessary truths reflect God's nature. Likewise, facts about what free creatures would do in any situation must be grounded in God's knowledge. So the structure of possibility points to God.`,
  short: `Several non-theistic accounts of modality are well developed: modal facts grounded in the essences of things (Kit Fine), in combinations of actual elements (Armstrong), or in the causal powers of the physical world (dispositional accounts). Theistic accounts face a Euthyphro-style dilemma: is something possible because God could make it, or could God make it because it's possible? If the first, possibility is arbitrary; if the second, possibility is prior to God. And the theistic account leaves God's own nature as an unexplained necessary fact, which is no better than a necessary physical foundation.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Essence-based modality", t: `Kit Fine ("Essence and Modality," 1994) argues that necessary truths are grounded in the essences of things: it's necessary that water is H2O because of what water is. On this view, modality doesn't need a mind. Contingent essences of contingent things ground contingent possibilities, and necessary truths about numbers are grounded in what numbers are.` },
    { h: "Power-based modality without God", t: `Pruss and Leftow ground possibility in God's powers. A naturalist can ground possibility in the powers of the physical world instead (Borghini & Williams, 2008; Jacobs, 2010; Vetter, *Potentiality*, 2015). This view has the same structure as the theistic account, minus the extra entity.` },
    { h: "The Euthyphro of modality", t: `Is a square circle impossible because God can't make one, or can God not make one because it's impossible? If the first, God's inability is just a brute fact about God. If the second, the impossibility is independent of God. Either way, the argument doesn't show that God is needed to ground modality.` },
    { h: "Counterfactuals of freedom", t: `Plantinga's argument from counterfactuals depends on there being true facts about what every possible free creature would do in every situation. Many philosophers, including many theists, deny that there are such facts. This is the "grounding objection" to Molinism (Adams, 1977; Hasker). See [[foreknowledge]].` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Leftow, B. (2012). God and Necessity. Oxford University Press.",
    "Pruss, A. (2011). Actuality, Possibility, and Worlds. Continuum.",
    "Fine, K. (1994). 'Essence and Modality.' Philosophical Perspectives 8: 1–16.",
    "Vetter, B. (2015). Potentiality: From Dispositions to Modality. Oxford University Press.",
    "Borghini, A. & Williams, N. (2008). 'A Dispositional Theory of Possibility.' Dialectica 62(1): 21–41.",
    "Adams, R. M. (1977). 'Middle Knowledge and the Problem of Evil.' American Philosophical Quarterly 14: 109–117."
  ],
  keys: "modality possibility necessity grounded in god possible worlds modal facts counterfactuals of freedom leftow pruss god's power",
  rel: ["abstract-objects", "contingency", "foreknowledge"]
},
{
  id: "a-priori",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Our Knowledge of Necessary Truths Points to God",
  who: "Plantinga's 'general argument from intuition' (argument Q); Paul Benacerraf's problem as used by theists (e.g., Plantinga, Where the Conflict Really Lies, 2011)",
  claim: `We know necessary truths, in logic and mathematics, by intuition, without experience. But if numbers and logical truths are abstract objects, they can't cause anything, so how could our brains, which interact only causally with the world, come to know them reliably? Naturalism has no explanation of this reliability. Theism does: God made our minds so that they can grasp the truths that are in his mind.`,
  short: `This is Paul Benacerraf's puzzle (1973), and it's a problem for one view of mathematics, platonism, not for naturalism in general. Nominalists and structuralists don't face it in the same form. Naturalists also offer explanations: evolution selects for abilities to track the structure of the world, and mathematics is the study of structure. Theism doesn't explain the mechanism either: how does a brain access a divine mind? "God arranged it" is a promissory note, not an explanation.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Benacerraf's problem is for platonists", t: `Benacerraf ("Mathematical Truth," 1973) argued that if mathematical objects are abstract, it's hard to see how we know about them. This is a reason some philosophers reject platonism, not a reason to adopt theism. Field and others use it as an argument for nominalism.` },
    { h: "Reliability can be explained", t: `Organisms that represent quantity, space, and logical relations accurately do better. Mathematics extends these abilities through deliberate, error-correcting practice. Justin Clarke-Doane (*Morality and Mathematics*, 2020) argues that the reliability challenge in mathematics can be met because mathematical truths are "safe": they couldn't easily have been different.` },
    { h: "Theism has the same gap", t: `If God designed our faculties to track truths in his mind, how does the tracking work? Human brains are causal, physical systems; the divine mind is non-physical. Theism places the mystery in a different location without solving it.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Benacerraf, P. (1973). 'Mathematical Truth.' Journal of Philosophy 70(19): 661–679.",
    "Field, H. (1989). Realism, Mathematics and Modality. Blackwell.",
    "Clarke-Doane, J. (2020). Morality and Mathematics. Oxford University Press.",
    "Dehaene, S. (2011). The Number Sense, rev. ed. Oxford University Press.",
    "Plantinga, A. (2011). Where the Conflict Really Lies. Oxford University Press."
  ],
  keys: "a priori knowledge necessary truths intuition mathematical knowledge benacerraf how do we know math abstract objects reliable god designed minds",
  rel: ["abstract-objects", "math-and-logic", "argument-from-reason"]
},
{
  id: "induction",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Only God Justifies Induction",
  who: "Plantinga's 'argument from induction' (argument M); presuppositional apologists (Greg Bahnsen); Genesis 8:22 ('while the earth remains... day and night shall not cease')",
  claim: `All science and everyday reasoning rely on induction: the assumption that the future will resemble the past. Hume showed that induction can't be justified without circularity. Naturalism gives no reason to expect nature to be uniform. Christianity does: God created an orderly world and promised its regularity. So only the theist can rationally rely on induction.`,
  short: `The problem of induction is unsolved for everyone. Theism doesn't solve it, because the theist must rely on induction to believe that God's past faithfulness and promises will continue, and must use inductive reasoning to read and trust scripture. A God who performs miracles is, by definition, a source of exceptions to uniformity. Philosophers have proposed non-theistic responses (pragmatic vindication, Bayesian approaches, the reliability of inductive practices), none of which require God.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Theism uses induction to justify induction", t: `"God will keep nature uniform because he promised to" relies on an inference from God's past reliability, and from scripture's past accuracy, to future reliability. That's inductive. Any appeal to God's character to secure induction is as circular as Hume said all such appeals are.` },
    { h: "Miracles undercut uniformity", t: `If God intervenes in nature at will, nature isn't guaranteed to be uniform. The theist must say God usually maintains uniformity, which is just as much an inductive generalization from experience as the naturalist's.` },
    { h: "Responses without God", t: `Hans Reichenbach argued that if any method can find regularities, induction will, so it's rational to use it (pragmatic vindication). Bayesian approaches show how evidence rationally updates beliefs. Reliabilists argue that inductive practices are justified if they're in fact reliable, whether or not we can prove it non-circularly. These are debated, but none needs God.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "God promised in Genesis 8:22 that seasons would continue. That grounds induction.", a: `You trust that promise because you trust scripture, based on past experience, testimony, or faith, so you're still using induction or assuming the conclusion. And Genesis 8:22 is about seasons, not about the uniformity of physical law. The same text describes a flood that violated every regularity of geology.`, k: "genesis 8:22 god promised seasons uniformity of nature" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Hume, D. (1748). An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, §IV.",
    "Reichenbach, H. (1938). Experience and Prediction. University of Chicago Press.",
    "Howson, C. (2000). Hume's Problem: Induction and the Justification of Belief. Oxford University Press.",
    "Henderson, L. 'The Problem of Induction.' Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy."
  ],
  keys: "induction problem of induction uniformity of nature future resembles past hume justify induction god guarantees regularity science assumes",
  rel: ["transcendental", "math-and-logic", "science-faith"]
},
{
  id: "meaning-reference",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Meaning and Reference Need a Divine Mind",
  who: "Plantinga's 'Putnamian argument', 'argument from reference', and 'Kripke-Wittgenstein argument from plus and quus' (arguments N, O, P); Saul Kripke, Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language (1982)",
  claim: `What makes my word "plus" mean addition rather than some bizarre function ("quus") that agrees with addition for every sum I've ever computed? Kripke's Wittgenstein argues that no fact about my past behavior or my brain fixes the meaning. Putnam argued that, without something to fix reference, our words could refer to almost anything. These problems are solved if God's intentions determine what our words and concepts mean.`,
  short: `The rule-following problem applies to God just as much: what makes God's concept "plus" mean addition rather than quaddition? If God's intentions fix meaning by being intrinsically determinate, the naturalist can say the same about human dispositions or about natural properties. David Lewis's "reference magnetism" solves Putnam's puzzle by appeal to natural kinds in the world, and dispositional and teleosemantic accounts address Kripke's puzzle. These are live philosophical problems, but adding God doesn't solve them.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The problem applies to God", t: `Kripke's skeptic asks what fact makes it the case that a thinker means plus. If the answer for God is "God just intends plus," then intentions are primitive meaning-fixers. A naturalist can make human intentions or dispositions, or natural properties, the meaning-fixers instead. The theist's solution only works by declaring the problem solved in one case.` },
    { h: "Reference magnetism", t: `David Lewis ("New Work for a Theory of Universals," 1983; "Putnam's Paradox," 1984) argued that some properties are more natural than others, and that these "magnetic" properties are eligible to be referred to. This blocks Putnam's model-theoretic argument without God. Ted Sider develops the view in *Writing the Book of the World* (2011).` },
    { h: "Dispositions and functions", t: `Responses to Kripke include dispositional accounts refined to handle errors, teleosemantic accounts (Millikan) on which meaning is fixed by biological function, and community-based accounts. The debate is ongoing, which is the normal state of philosophy, not a sign that God is needed.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Kripke, S. (1982). Wittgenstein on Rules and Private Language. Harvard University Press.",
    "Putnam, H. (1981). Reason, Truth and History. Cambridge University Press.",
    "Lewis, D. (1983). 'New Work for a Theory of Universals.' Australasian Journal of Philosophy 61(4): 343–377.",
    "Lewis, D. (1984). 'Putnam's Paradox.' Australasian Journal of Philosophy 62(3): 221–236.",
    "Sider, T. (2011). Writing the Book of the World. Oxford University Press.",
    "Walls, J. & Dougherty, T. (eds.) (2018). Two Dozen (or so) Arguments for God. Oxford University Press."
  ],
  keys: "meaning reference plus quus kripke wittgenstein rule following putnam paradox what fixes meaning words refer god's intentions",
  rel: ["intentionality", "abstract-objects", "argument-from-reason"]
},
{
  id: "transcendental",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Presuppositionalism: The Transcendental Argument",
  who: "Cornelius Van Til; Greg Bahnsen (debate with Gordon Stein, 1985); Sye Ten Bruggencate; Jeff Durbin",
  claim: `Logic, science, and morality all presuppose the Christian God. The unbeliever cannot "account for" the laws of logic, the uniformity of nature, or moral absolutes within their own worldview. So every time they reason, they borrow from the Christian worldview. The proof of God is the impossibility of the contrary. Any attempt to argue against God uses tools only God provides. "How do you know your reasoning is valid? Could you be wrong about everything you know?"`,
  short: `Transcendental arguments need to show that *only* the Christian God could make logic, induction, and morality possible. That has never been done. Any being or necessary structure stipulated to "ground" these would do equally well (Martin's parody). The "how do you know" regress applies equally to the theist, whose claim to know God via revelation relies on their own reasoning to interpret that revelation. And "God revealed it so I can be certain" is circular by the presuppositionalist's own admission.`,
  points: [
    { h: "The 'impossibility of the contrary' is asserted, not shown", t: `To prove that God is the *necessary* precondition of logic, you would need to refute every alternative: logic as necessary in itself, as a feature of language, as a description of all possible worlds, or as grounded in a non-Christian deity. Presuppositionalists rarely consider these. They demand that the opponent "account for" logic, then declare victory. A Muslim, a Hindu, or a believer in a "logic-god" can run the identical argument.` },
    { h: "God doesn't ground logic either", t: `If logic is grounded in God's nature, why is God's nature logical? Either because it must be, in which case logic is prior, or because God is just that way, a brute fact the presuppositionalist says the atheist cannot have. Michael Martin's "Transcendental Argument for the Non-existence of God" (1996) argues that if God created logic, God could have made it otherwise, which undermines its necessity. So logic's necessity tells *against* a creator of logic.` },
    { h: "Circular by design", t: `Bahnsen accepted that his argument is circular, but called it "virtuously" circular because all ultimate commitments are. If circularity is acceptable at the foundations, then the naturalist can be equally "virtuously" circular about logic and the senses, and the argument proves nothing. The demand for absolute certainty also defeats itself: the believer must be certain that their interpretation of revelation is correct, using the same fallible mind.` },
    { h: "Induction is a problem for everyone", t: `Hume's problem of induction (why expect the future to resemble the past?) is unsolved for everyone. Saying "God promised nature would be uniform" (citing Genesis 8:22) relies on the past reliability of God's promises, which is itself an inductive inference. It also sits poorly with a God who performs miracles, which are by definition breaks in uniformity.` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "Could you be wrong about everything you know? If so, how do you know anything?", a: `I could be wrong about any particular thing; that's what it means to be fallible. Knowledge doesn't require certainty. It requires reliable belief formation, which is open to checking. The question works equally on you: could you be wrong about God's revelation? If you say no, because God revealed it to you, you are using your own fallible mind to judge that he did. Infallibility is unavailable to anyone.`, k: "could you be wrong about everything know anything certainty how do you know presupp sye" },
    { q: "You're borrowing from the Christian worldview when you use logic.", a: `Logic was formalized by Aristotle three centuries before Christianity, and Indian (Nyāya) and Chinese (Mohist) logicians worked independently. It is used by people of every religion and none, all of whom would then be "borrowing" from their own worldviews. The claim is an assertion that the Christian God is the only possible ground of logic, which is the very thing that needed proof.`, k: "borrowing christian worldview using logic presuppose god account for" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Bahnsen, G. vs. Stein, G. (1985). 'The Great Debate: Does God Exist?' University of California, Irvine.",
    "Van Til, C. (1955). The Defense of the Faith. Presbyterian & Reformed.",
    "Martin, M. (1996). 'The Transcendental Argument for the Nonexistence of God.' The Secular Web.",
    "Stroud, B. (1968). 'Transcendental Arguments.' Journal of Philosophy 65(9): 241–256.",
    "Hume, D. (1748). An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, §IV."
  ],
  keys: "presuppositionalism transcendental argument tag account for logic impossibility of the contrary bahnsen van til sye ten bruggencate how do you know borrowing worldview uniformity of nature",
  rel: ["math-and-logic", "argument-from-reason", "faith"]
},
{
  id: "lucas-penrose",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Gödel's Theorem Shows the Mind Isn't a Machine",
  who: "J. R. Lucas, 'Minds, Machines and Gödel' (1961); Roger Penrose, The Emperor's New Mind (1989) and Shadows of the Mind (1994); used by apologists for the immaterial mind",
  claim: `Gödel proved that any consistent formal system strong enough for arithmetic contains a true statement it can't prove. But a human mathematician can see that the statement is true. So the human mind can do something no machine (no formal system) can do, which means the mind isn't a physical computing mechanism. This supports an immaterial soul and a creator of rational minds.`,
  short: `Logicians have largely rejected the argument. Gödel's theorem applies to *consistent* systems, and we can only "see" a system's Gödel sentence is true if we know the system is consistent. Humans can't establish their own consistency, and we make mathematical errors. Hilary Putnam, Solomon Feferman, and Paul Benacerraf identified these flaws. Penrose himself is not a theist: his conclusion is that we need new physics, not a soul.`,
  points: [
    { h: "We don't know our own consistency", t: `The Gödel sentence of a system S is true only if S is consistent. To "see" that it's true, a human must know that S, possibly the system that describes their own reasoning, is consistent. Gödel's second incompleteness theorem shows that a consistent system can't prove its own consistency. Humans are no better placed: we have no proof that our own mathematical reasoning is consistent (Putnam, 1995; Benacerraf, 1967).` },
    { h: "Humans aren't infallible", t: `Human mathematicians make mistakes and have sometimes accepted inconsistent principles (Frege's Basic Law V, which led to Russell's paradox). A fallible reasoner isn't a consistent formal system, so Gödel's theorem doesn't straightforwardly apply to human reasoning, and neither does Lucas's argument.` },
    { h: "Penrose's conclusion isn't theism", t: `Penrose argues that consciousness involves non-computable physical processes, perhaps related to quantum gravity. He doesn't argue for a soul or for God. Even if the Lucas–Penrose argument worked, it would show that minds aren't Turing machines, not that they're immaterial.` },
    { h: "Expert verdict", t: `Solomon Feferman ("Penrose's Gödelian Argument," 1995) and other logicians analyzed the argument in detail and found errors in its logic. It is generally regarded in philosophy of mathematics as unsound.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Lucas, J. R. (1961). 'Minds, Machines and Gödel.' Philosophy 36: 112–127.",
    "Penrose, R. (1994). Shadows of the Mind. Oxford University Press.",
    "Benacerraf, P. (1967). 'God, the Devil, and Gödel.' The Monist 51(1): 9–32.",
    "Putnam, H. (1995). 'Review of Shadows of the Mind.' Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society 32(3): 370–373.",
    "Feferman, S. (1995). 'Penrose's Gödelian Argument.' Psyche 2.",
    "Franzén, T. (2005). Gödel's Theorem: An Incomplete Guide to Its Use and Abuse. A K Peters."
  ],
  keys: "godel incompleteness theorem mind not machine lucas penrose human mind can see truth computers can't soul immaterial mind",
  rel: ["consciousness", "argument-from-reason", "soul"]
},
{
  id: "free-will-soul",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "Free Will Requires a Soul (and Naturalism Destroys Responsibility)",
  who: "J. P. Moreland; Richard Swinburne, Mind, Brain, and Free Will (2013); popular apologetics ('if we're just atoms, we're not responsible for anything')",
  claim: `We have genuine free will: we're the originators of our choices and responsible for them. If naturalism were true, every choice would be fully determined by physical causes, or random, and neither is free. So naturalism destroys free will, moral responsibility, and even rational deliberation. Libertarian free will requires an immaterial self that can initiate actions, which fits a world created by a free God.`,
  short: `A soul faces the same dilemma: a soul's choices are either caused by its prior states or uncaused, and either way the same worries arise (Hume's fork applies to souls too). Most philosophers are compatibilists, who hold that freedom and responsibility are compatible with determinism; in the 2020 PhilPapers survey, about 59% accepted or leaned toward compatibilism. And Christian theology has its own threats to free will: divine foreknowledge and, for Calvinists, predestination.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Souls don't escape the dilemma", t: `If a soul chooses because of its desires, character, and reasons, its choice is determined by those prior states. If it chooses without any cause, the choice is random. Libertarians propose "agent causation," but whatever that is, it isn't explained by adding non-physical stuff. A physical agent could equally be an agent cause (O'Connor, *Persons and Causes*, 2000, defends agent causation within a broadly naturalistic framework).` },
    { h: "Compatibilism is the majority view", t: `Compatibilists (Frankfurt, Dennett, Fischer) argue that freedom is acting on your own reasons and desires, without coercion, with responsiveness to reasons. Responsibility fits this. In the 2020 PhilPapers survey, roughly 59% of philosophers accepted or leaned toward compatibilism, and only about 19% toward libertarianism.` },
    { h: "Theism's own threats to freedom", t: `If God infallibly foreknows every choice, or (for Calvinists) foreordains them, the theist faces a version of the determinism problem (see [[foreknowledge]]). Augustine, Luther, Calvin, and Jonathan Edwards denied libertarian freedom on theological grounds.` },
    { h: "Responsibility doesn't need metaphysical origination", t: `Our practices of praise and blame respond to people's intentions, reasons, and capacities. These are features of persons whether persons are physical or not (Strawson, "Freedom and Resentment," 1962).` }
  ],
  replies: [
    { q: "If we're just atoms following physics, then your argument is just atoms too, and there's no reason to believe it.", a: `Atoms organized into a calculator produce correct sums; atoms organized into a brain can produce correct inferences. Whether a belief is justified depends on how it's formed and whether it tracks evidence, not on what it's made of. See [[argument-from-reason]].`, k: "just atoms physics argument just atoms reason determined" }
  ],
  sources: [
    "Swinburne, R. (2013). Mind, Brain, and Free Will. Oxford University Press.",
    "Dennett, D. (2003). Freedom Evolves. Viking.",
    "Fischer, J. M. & Ravizza, M. (1998). Responsibility and Control. Cambridge University Press.",
    "O'Connor, T. (2000). Persons and Causes: The Metaphysics of Free Will. Oxford University Press.",
    "Strawson, P. F. (1962). 'Freedom and Resentment.' Proceedings of the British Academy 48: 1–25.",
    "Bourget, D. & Chalmers, D. (2023). 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey.' Philosophers' Imprint."
  ],
  keys: "free will requires soul libertarian free will determinism naturalism destroys responsibility just atoms choice agent causation moral responsibility",
  rel: ["soul", "foreknowledge", "argument-from-reason", "consciousness"]
},
{
  id: "colors-flavors",
  cat: "mind",
  title: "The Argument from Colors and Flavors",
  who: "Robert Merrihew Adams, 'Flavors, Colors, and God' (in The Virtue of Faith, 1987); Plantinga's argument S; Richard Swinburne",
  claim: `Why does a particular brain state feel like the taste of pineapple, rather than like the color green or nothing at all? The correlations between physical states and specific sensations seem arbitrary: physics doesn't predict them. A personal God, who chose these correlations for our good and enjoyment, explains why they exist and are stable. Naturalism leaves them a brute mystery.`,
  short: `This is a version of the hard problem of consciousness, and the same answer applies: an unexplained correlation isn't evidence for one supernatural explanation. "God chose it" fits any correlation whatsoever, so it predicts nothing. Meanwhile, the structure of our sensory experience is increasingly explained by neuroscience. The fact that red and green are opponent colors, for instance, reflects the opponent-process wiring of the visual system. And the correlations aren't arbitrary in the way the argument needs: they track the functional organization of perception.`,
  points: [
    { h: "Structure tracks the brain", t: `Color experience has a structure: hues form a circle, red and green are opposites, some colors are "unique" while others look mixed. These facts reflect opponent-process coding in the visual system (Hering; Hurvich & Jameson). C. L. Hardin (*Color for Philosophers*, 1988) argues that the phenomenology of color tracks the neural coding. That's an explanation of why experiences relate as they do, not a brute correlation.` },
    { h: "'God chose it' predicts nothing", t: `If God chose that pineapple tastes as it does, he could have chosen anything. A hypothesis compatible with every possible correlation receives no support from any particular one (see [[unfalsifiable]]).` },
    { h: "Same problem for theism", t: `If God's choice explains why a brain state feels a certain way, how does a non-physical decision attach an experience to a neural pattern? Theism faces its own version of the hard problem: how divine willing produces and sustains specific experiences.` }
  ],
  replies: [],
  sources: [
    "Adams, R. M. (1987). 'Flavors, Colors, and God.' In The Virtue of Faith. Oxford University Press.",
    "Hardin, C. L. (1988). Color for Philosophers: Unweaving the Rainbow. Hackett.",
    "Hurvich, L. & Jameson, D. (1957). 'An Opponent-Process Theory of Color Vision.' Psychological Review 64(6): 384–404.",
    "Chalmers, D. (1996). The Conscious Mind. Oxford University Press."
  ],
  keys: "colors flavors qualia taste of pineapple why does red look red sensations correlations god chose secondary qualities adams",
  rel: ["consciousness", "disembodied-mind", "beauty"]
}
]);
